{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788963977,"nonce":"oPj/ZA0AucMi2r9td3ELnAZVPmY=","message":"FIRST HALF LANDED, ACCEPTANCE UNDISCHARGED. 8725cc2 on main, rebuilt in the commit\nafter it. RimWorld was never launched; none of the 74 steps in\naccept/975973e-screen.md has been run.\n\nThis issue was split because it is too large for one pass. What landed is the screen\nOBJECT and its six panels, which adds a reply and changes NO control flow. The answer\ngate, `defer` and `write-off` are the second half and are deliberately absent — the\nworker confirms no seam forced it to reach into them, and `decisions.gate` says so on\nevery screen.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT MATTERS, and it contradicts the design. COCKPIT.md budgets the\nscreen at two to four thousand tokens per turn. Measured against this run's own colony\nat the raid that ended it — 7 colonists, 7 turrets with 1 lost, 4 live alerts, 3\nunreachable batteries, 2 decisions served of 4 owed, 31 rows since the last screen:\n\n  realistic  13,925 bytes  ~3,500-4,600 tokens   at the top of budget, 3.5x the design's byte estimate\n  saturated  25,409 bytes  ~6,400-8,500 tokens   OVER\n\nFirst assembly was 17,054 bytes, 6,538 of them fixed prose. The worker cut the\nscreen's copies of the digest disclaimers to one line each, hoisted the alert-age note\noff twelve rows (3 KB), collapsed six per-goal `set` lines into one, and sized the\ncaps against the measurement rather than by taste. That is what got it to 13,925.\n\nIts recommendation if a panel must go: `goals`, 1,757 bytes, the only block that is a\ncontract rather than an observation and the one that changes on the scale of in-game\ndays. It did NOT cut it, because \"all six graded on every screen\" is the spec's own\nwords. That is a decision for Dorian, not a worker.\n\nDECISIONS ARE SERVED ONE KIND AT A TIME, with `groups_pending`, `total_owed` and\n`by_kind` beside them — which is element 1's amendment from the walkthrough, arriving\nhere rather than in the gate. A note for the second-half worker: the gate should\nrefuse naming `owed[].id`, the SERVED group, not the whole pile. Serving all owed ids\nat once is a two-line change to ScreenDecisions.Panel if half two wants it.\n\nTWO GOALS GRADE `ok: null` — UNKNOWN, never false: G3's armour clause (47547ca is\nopen) and G4 entirely (f1a1700 #2, no durable baseline across a load). Grading them\nfalse would have been the honest-instruments defect this design exists to prevent.\n\nTHE LAST-SCREEN MARK ACROSS A LOAD needed a second flag. `lastScreenSeq` is already\ncleared in Abandon, but ClockSample SEEDS it on a game's first frame, so a non-zero\nmark does not mean a screen went out. `everDeliveredScreen` is separate, cleared in\nAbandon and set at the BOTTOM of BuildData — not in Teardown, which runs first and\nwould have meant the first screen after a load never said \"no earlier screen\".\nLossLevels, ScreenLog and the save mark are cleared in Runtime.ResetForGameBoundary so\nboth boundary detectors reach them.\n\nA `look` also delivers a screen and moves the mark, per 65e7cf9's definition, and\nprovably cannot create a read obligation: 722c951 keys on lastAdvanceEndSeq against\nJournal.ReadWatermark, and neither is touched.\n\n`LossLevels.Release` is the seam `write-off` will call. Nothing calls it yet, so a\nloss level persists for the life of the game — the safe direction, and stated in the\ndata as `losses.clears_with`. A rebuilt turret still clears its own level by being\ncounted; Release is only for deciding NOT to rebuild.\n\nONE PRE-EXISTING SUITE FAILURE, proven not a regression:\naccept/1113019-until-bound.py --selftest 9.2c greps for a literal\n`ErrUnreachableHalt = \"unreachable-halt\"` while the source ships\n`ErrCode.Refused(\"unreachable-halt\")`. That line is untouched on this branch and the\ncheck fails identically on the commit before it. The suite is wrong, not the mod.\n\nGreen on the merged tree: s13-mod-surface --selftest 38/38, b1b3060-posture --selftest\n26/26, rwa/selftest.sh 263/263.\n\nSTILL OWED: all of accept/975973e-screen.md. Its §I is the control-flow check to run\nFIRST if anything looks wrong — advance must still not refuse with `decision-owed`,\n`defer` and `write-off` must not exist, the 722c951 gate must be unchanged, and all\n~21 pre-existing advance fields must still be present. And the byte measurement above\nis synthetic until §J runs it against a real screen.","files":null}]}